<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Utilities</h1>
<p class="block-content">
PRADO includes utility classes for common tasks. This page describes the ones an
application uses directly.
</p>

<h2>Graceful exit</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TExitException</tt> ends the application from anywhere in the request. The application
raises <tt>onEndRequest</tt> and exits with the exit code that the exception carries, and
it does not raise <tt>onError</tt>. The
<a href="?page=Fundamentals.Applications">application lifecycle</a> page describes the
behavior.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Exceptions\TExitException;

throw new TExitException(0, 'Done.');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Web color names</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TWebColor</tt> defines the extended web color names and their hex values.
<tt>TPropertyValue::ensureHexColor</tt> converts a color name to its hex value, so a
property accepts a name or a hex value. The name is not case-sensitive.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\TPropertyValue;

TPropertyValue::ensureHexColor('RebeccaPurple');   // '#663399'
TPropertyValue::ensureHexColor('SteelBlue');       // '#4682B4'
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TWebColors</tt>, with the plural name, is the same list under an older name. It is a
deprecated stub that will be removed, so new code uses <tt>TWebColor</tt>.
</p>

<h2>Rational numbers</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TRational</tt> holds a number as one integer divided by another, which is how EXIF and
TIFF record values such as an exposure time or a GPS coordinate. <tt>TURational</tt> is the
unsigned form. Reading such a value as a float loses the pair the file actually stored, and
these classes keep it.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
A value is set from a string in <tt>numerator/denominator</tt> form, from an array of the
two integers, or from a float. A float is converted with a continued fraction to a stated
tolerance, so the pair is the closest one within that tolerance rather than an arbitrary
rounding.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Util\Math\TRational;

$shutter = new TRational('1/250');
echo $shutter-&gt;getValue();        // 0.004
echo $shutter-&gt;getNumerator();    // 1
echo $shutter-&gt;getDenominator();  // 250
echo $shutter;                    // '1/250'

$approx = new TRational(0.375);
echo $approx;                     // '3/8'
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
These classes do not extend <tt>TComponent</tt>, so they have no property syntax. Call the
getters rather than reading <tt>Value</tt> or <tt>Numerator</tt> as properties.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The numerator and the denominator are also reachable by index, at <tt>[0]</tt> and
<tt>[1]</tt> or by the names <tt>numerator</tt> and <tt>denominator</tt>. Infinity is held
as <tt>-1/0</tt>, and a value that is not a number has a zero denominator, so neither case
raises a division error.
</p>

<h2>Extracting tar archives</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TTarFileExtractor</tt> reads a tar archive, including one compressed with gzip, bzip2
or xz, and writes it into a destination directory.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\IO\TTarFileExtractor;

$extractor = new TTarFileExtractor('/path/to/archive.tar.gz');
$extractor-&gt;extract('/destination/directory');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The archive path may also be an HTTP or HTTPS URL, which the extractor downloads first.
<tt>UrlTimeout</tt> sets how long it waits.
</p>

<h3>Treating the archive as untrusted</h3>

<p class="block-content">
An archive can name entries that write outside the directory you gave it, or links that
point outside it. <tt>Strict</tt> decides what happens then, and it defaults to true.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th><tt>Strict</tt></th><th>On a security violation</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>true</td>
	<td>Extraction stops and throws. Nothing further is written.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>false</td>
	<td>The offending entry is skipped and the rest is extracted. <tt>getSkippedFiles()</tt> lists what was passed over, and <tt>hasSkippedFiles()</tt> answers whether anything was.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Existing files</h3>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>ConflictMode</tt> decides what an entry does when a file of that name already exists.
It defaults to overwriting.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Mode</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CONFLICT_OVERWRITE</tt></td><td>Replace the existing file. This is the default.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CONFLICT_SKIP</tt></td><td>Keep the existing file and record the entry as skipped.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CONFLICT_ERROR</tt></td><td>Stop and throw.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CONFLICT_NEWER</tt></td><td>Replace only when the archived entry is newer.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>CONFLICT_OLDER</tt></td><td>Replace only when the archived entry is older.</td></tr>
<tr><td>a callable</td><td>Decide per entry. Returning an empty result skips the entry, and a type error inside the callable skips it as well.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>All or nothing</h3>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>Atomic</tt> defaults to false, so a failure part way through leaves whatever was
already written in place. Setting it to true stages the files in a private temporary
directory and merges them into the destination at the end, backing up anything it
overwrites so the destination can be restored if the merge fails. The guarantee costs
extra disk space and a second pass over the data.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$extractor = new TTarFileExtractor('/path/to/archive.tar.gz');
$extractor-&gt;setAtomic(true)
          -&gt;setConflictMode(TTarFileExtractor::CONFLICT_NEWER);
$extractor-&gt;extract('/destination/directory');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>DirModeOverride</tt> and <tt>FileModeOverride</tt> force permissions on everything
written, in place of the modes the archive carries.
</p>

<h3>Inspecting before extracting</h3>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>getManifest()</tt> reads the archive without writing anything. Each entry reports its
path, size, modification time, mode and type. An entry that would not be extracted carries
a <tt>reason</tt>, and one rejected on security grounds carries a <tt>security</tt> key
naming the violation, such as an attempt to escape the destination. That key is what
separates a security rejection from an ordinary file conflict.
</p>

<h2>Processes and signals</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TProcessHelper</tt> provides static methods for a command-line process. It forks a
process with <tt>fork</tt>, tests whether a process is running with <tt>isRunning</tt>,
escapes a shell argument with <tt>escapeShellArg</tt>, and reports the operating system
with <tt>isSystemLinux</tt> and <tt>isSystemMacOS</tt>. A long-running process, such as the
<a href="?page=Advanced.Cron">cron</a> scheduler, uses these methods.
</p>
<h3>Handling signals</h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TSignalsDispatcher</tt> turns an operating-system signal into a PRADO global event.
When a signal arrives, the dispatcher raises the matching <tt>fx</tt> event, and the
attached handlers run. A long-running process, such as the
<a href="?page=Advanced.Cron">cron</a> scheduler, handles a signal so it shuts down in
order. Signal handling requires the PHP process-control extension.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The dispatcher is a singleton. The <tt>singleton</tt> method returns it, and the
<tt>attach</tt> method installs it as the handler of the signals. The <tt>detach</tt>
method removes it and restores the handlers that were in place before.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Util\TSignalsDispatcher;

$dispatcher = TSignalsDispatcher::singleton();
$dispatcher->attach();   // install the signal handlers

$dispatcher->attachEventHandler('fxSignalInterrupt', function ($sender, $param) {
    $param->setExit(false);   // do not exit on this signal
    // stop the work in an orderly way
});
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Each signal maps to an event. The common signals map as the table shows. The less common signals are not shown here.
</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Signal</th><th>Event</th><th>Exits by default</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGINT</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalInterrupt</tt></td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGTERM</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalTerminate</tt></td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGHUP</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalHangUp</tt></td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGQUIT</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalQuit</tt></td><td>yes</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGUSR1</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalUser1</tt></td><td>no</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGUSR2</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalUser2</tt></td><td>no</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGALRM</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalAlarm</tt></td><td>no</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>SIGCHLD</tt></td><td><tt>fxSignalChild</tt></td><td>no</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="block-content">
A signal that exits by default ends the process after the handlers run. A handler stops
the exit by calling <tt>setExit(false)</tt> on the event parameter, so the process
continues. This lets a process treat a stop request as a request to finish its current
work first.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The dispatcher also schedules alarms and tracks child processes. The static <tt>alarm</tt>
method adds a timed callback that raises <tt>fxSignalAlarm</tt> when it is due, and
<tt>disarm</tt> removes it. A handler attached with <tt>attachPidHandler</tt> runs when a
specific child process ends, which the dispatcher routes from <tt>SIGCHLD</tt>.
</p>

<h2>One-time initialization</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TInitializedTrait</tt> gives a class a one-time initialization guard. The
<tt>getIsInitialized</tt> method reports whether initialization has run, and
<tt>markInitialized</tt> records that it has. The <tt>assertUninitialized</tt> method
throws when a property is set after initialization, so a module rejects a configuration
change that comes too late. The parameter and cron modules use the trait.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
public function setConnectionID($value)
{
    $this->assertUninitialized('ConnectionID');   // throws if already initialized
    $this->_connectionID = $value;
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

</com:TContent>
